fit of hindsight  now as to kind of the intentions and i'm  wondering in terms of tactics i mean  think sometimes in environmental  protests there's the  chaining oneself to x and sometimes it's  a tree or sometimes it's the fence at a  nuclear power plant or whatever the case  may be does the tactic itself tell you  much about  the the moral concern  yeah i think the tactic tells you a lot  about the moral concern so for instance  if the tactic this may be too obvious to  say but if the tactic is violent  it automatically  delegitimates the moral concern in most  people's eyes okay it's like if you're  even you know i guess this is a  unless  right so if you're just a terrorist  if you're an environmental terrorist and  you shoot somebody no one is going very  few people are going to be persuaded  right certainly congress is not going to  be persuaded  by that kind of a protest if your tactic  is non-violent  if you spell out exactly  what your principles are and why you're  violating some law  then  that demonstrates a kind of moral  seriousness and that's an actual appeal  to the public  right and and um moreover  to be willing to  suffer on on behalf of your principles  like to to  martin luther king was so committed to  non-violence  in a way that requires such extreme  restraint  right to literally be beaten and saying  that's the ultimate demonstration of  moral seriousness like i'm so serious  about this moral principle and getting  it in the right means that i'm willing  to subject myself to this  that was extremely persuasive to people  absolutely fascinating topic we've been  speaking with coleman hughes you can  check out coleman's podcast  conversations with coleman on which i am  a forthcoming guest uh coleman always  great chatting with you i appreciate  your time anytime david
 

